# Secret read from a NEXT_PUBLIC_* environment variable. Next.js INLINES every
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* value into the client JS bundle at build time, so anything named
# like a secret/token/key/password there ships to every browser in plaintext.
#
# Found as a real keur false-negative (2026-09-26) via the author's own marker:
#   mestjs web/src/lib/api.ts:7
#   const API_TOKEN = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_SECRET ?? 'fallback-token'
#   // INTENTIONAL (MEST-NEXT-001): a secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* var
# No existing keur rule covered the Next.js public-env-var exposure class.
#
# Match a NEXT_PUBLIC_ env var whose name looks like a credential
# (SECRET/TOKEN/KEY/PASSWORD/API...). A NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE url does NOT match.
id: KEUR-NEXT-001
maturity: 2
title: Secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable — inlined into the client bundle
category: secret
severity: error
engine: pattern
action: block
target:
  extensions: .ts .tsx .js .jsx .mjs
patterns:
  - regex: (?i)NEXT_PUBLIC_[A-Z0-9_]*(?:SECRET|TOKEN|API_KEY|APIKEY|PASSWORD|PASSWD|PRIVATE|CREDENTIAL|AUTH_KEY)
    message: A NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable named like a secret is inlined into the client bundle at build (CWE-200). Next.js ships every NEXT_PUBLIC_* value to the browser — never put a secret/token/key there. Read secrets server-side (a non-NEXT_PUBLIC_ var) only.
confidence: 0.85
fix: Move the secret to a server-only env var (drop the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix) and access it in a server component / route handler / API route — never in client code.
